        "## **Model Building**"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Dg8Mx5keJxQr"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### **Resampling**"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "A9jpxObfKzn2"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- Before we start building our models, let us create resampled versions (undersampled and oversampled) of our training dataset as the distribution of our dependant variable is uneven (the parent dataset is an imbalanced dataset).\n",
        "\n",
        "- These resampled datasets will help us compare model performances across them to see which models perform better when trained on which datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "- To understand which metric might be of importance to us in evaluating the models, let's take a look at the implications of the dependant variable. If the OFLC (Office of Foreign Labour Certification) denies a deserving application, that is opportunity loss. However, if the OFLC approves a problematic application, that stands to cause damage to the country. Hence, false positives are more expensive over here. We will therefore go with Specificity (predicted negetives or 0s over actual negetives or 0s) as an appropriate measure of performance in evaluating our models.\n",
        "\n",
        "- Drawing from the previous observation, it is more important for the models to correctly predict the applications that would be denied (0s, the minority class) than it is to predict the applications that would be approved (1s, the majority class)."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "z4fbcIVOcSQ-"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# First, let's start by looking at the imbalance of the dependant variable:\n",
        "\n",
        "y.value_counts(1)"
      ],
