        "**Observations from comparison and final model selection:**\n",
        "\n",
        "- When we look at the initial models prior to tuning, we see that the Decision Tree Classifier and Random Forest Classifier are overfit on the training dataset. This is due to the fact that they contain fully grown decision trees or forests. While the three boosting models also work using decision trees, we don't see overfitting taking place with them as they are boosting models.\n",
        "\n",
        "- Amongst the initial models, we see that the Gradient Boosting model and the Bagging Classifier model perform the best across all three datasets (original, oversampled, and undersampled), although the margin by which they outperform the other models is not significant.\n",
        "\n",
        "- When we look at the tuned models, we see that the Bagging Classifier model and the Random Forest model perform the best amonst models trained on the original training dataset, while amongst models trained on the resampled training datasets, the XG Boosting models outperforms both.\n",
        "\n",
        "- Generally speaking, the margin by which the performance of the models (initial as well as tuned) differ from one another is not very pronounced. This is understandable as with the exception of the Decision Tree Classifier, all other models are ensemble models.\n",
        "\n",
        "- When we look at tuned models alongside initial models, we see that the tuned models perform better, although again, by a very small margin. This can again be attributed to the fact that they are all ensemble models (except Decision Tree Classifier) that are already robust due to their construction prior to tuning.\n",
        "\n",
        "- When we look at models across the three datasets (original, oversampled, and undersampled), we see an overall pattern wherein performance is lowest amongst models trained on the original training dataset, slightly better amongst models trained on the oversampled training dataset, and best amongst models trained on the undersampled training dataset. This might be attributed to the fact that it is more important for the model to predict the negetives (or 0s), and therefore it gives us more precise results when trained on datasets where the positives have been adjusted to align with the negetives (undersampling).\n",
        "\n",
        "- Across all models listed across all three comparison tables, we see that the Tuned XG Boosting model trained on the undersampled training dataset performs the best, considering it has the highest Specificities (our primary metric of interest) of all models while also having reasonably high Accuracies. Both its Specificities as well as Accuracies are reasonably in the proximity of one another across the training and validation datasets, suggesting that the models are not overfit either. **Hence, we will conclude to select the Tuned XG Boosting model trained on the undersampled training dataset as our final model of prediction.**"
